# clang-tidy configuration for Assurance Forge (issue #293).
#
# Selection principle: a check earns its place by finding defects in THIS
# codebase, not by being enabled in someone else's. Every family below was run
# over all 255 production translation units before being kept or dropped, and
# every exclusion names what it actually fired on. An unexplained exclusion is
# indistinguishable from a check nobody dared turn on.
#
# The enabled set produced 90 findings when first measured, and 69 now that
# issue #306 has closed the 21 describing correctness problems. The four
# families with no exclusions produce 4,784, and the nine exclusions below
# account for 4,694 of them — 98%, with five checks alone contributing 4,586.
# Baselining all of it would have meant baselining noise, and a baseline that
# is mostly noise is one nobody reads.
#
# Re-derive these counts rather than trusting them:
#   python tools/quality/run_clang_tidy.py --all-checks
#
# Rationale in prose, with the measured counts:
#   docs/quality/static-analysis.md
#
# Run it with:
#   python tools/quality/run_clang_tidy.py

Checks: >
  -*,
  bugprone-*,
  clang-analyzer-*,
  performance-*,
  misc-*,
  -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,
  -bugprone-exception-escape,
  -clang-analyzer-optin.performance.Padding,
  -misc-include-cleaner,
  -misc-const-correctness,
  -misc-no-recursion,
  -misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes,
  -misc-use-anonymous-namespace,
  -performance-enum-size

# Why each exclusion, with the count it contributed to the unfiltered 4,784:
#
#   misc-include-cleaner (3,357) — demands include-what-you-use discipline and
#     would rewrite nearly every include block. CLAUDE.md states the opposite
#     policy: SortIncludes is disabled and include order is preserved by hand,
#     because Windows and some third-party headers have order dependencies.
#     Enforcing this check would break the build it is meant to protect.
#
#   misc-const-correctness (756) — flags locals that could be const. Real but
#     purely stylistic, and 756 edits across every subsystem is exactly the
#     "broad stylistic churn" #293 exists to avoid. A candidate for a later,
#     deliberate pass; not for the first baseline.
#
#   misc-non-private-member-variables-in-classes (241) — ui::UiState,
#     core::AppState and the SACM element structs are deliberately public data
#     aggregates. Making them private would add accessors that carry no
#     invariant, which is not an improvement.
#
#   bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters (141) — flags any two adjacent
#     same-typed parameters. Its own documentation concedes a high false
#     positive rate, and here it fires on (id, name) string pairs throughout
#     the model, where the names already distinguish them.
#
#   performance-enum-size (91) — asks for smaller underlying enum types. A
#     micro-optimization with no measured motivation in a GUI application.
#
#   bugprone-exception-escape (50) — measured, not assumed: it fires on the
#     implicitly-generated move constructors of aggregate structs holding
#     std::string/std::vector (SacmElement, MultiLangText, ReviewProposal ...)
#     and on main(). It found no hand-written noexcept function that can throw,
#     which is the defect the check is for.
#
#   misc-no-recursion (28) — this codebase walks trees (assurance tree, GSN
#     layout, XMI element nesting). Recursion is the design, not an accident.
#
#   misc-use-anonymous-namespace (28) — flags `static` at file scope where the
#     function already sits inside an anonymous namespace. Removing a redundant
#     but harmless keyword at 28 sites is naming-only churn.
#
#   clang-analyzer-optin.performance.Padding (2) — reports struct field ordering
#     for padding. Reordering ui::UiState's 38 fields to save bytes would
#     scramble a struct people read, for memory nobody has measured a need for.

# Third-party code is passed with -isystem by the runner, so it is suppressed
# before this filter applies. This keeps first-party headers reported once
# rather than once per translation unit that includes them.
HeaderFilterRegex: '(^|[/\\])(src|libs)[/\\]'

# Findings are recorded and ratcheted, never auto-fixed. A tool rewriting
# safety-case handling code unattended is not a trade this project makes.
FormatStyle: none
WarningsAsErrors: ''
